WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.055 --> 00:00:05.954
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.954 --> 00:00:09.700
4:30 PM on a Thursday afternoon
in Johannesburg, South Africa.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:15.616
It's 2008, and we're in the midst
of a global financial crisis,

00:00:15.616 --> 00:00:18.757
the largest one since the dot-com
bubble in the year 2000.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:25.213
Rumors are swirling about
the global economy collapsing,

00:00:25.213 --> 00:00:29.750
about banks going bankrupt,
about people losing their jobs.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:35.320
I'm a commodity trader in
an investment bank, and

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.370
with only 30 minutes to go until
the market closes, I can't wait,

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:39.720
because phones around me
are ringing uncontrollably and

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.489
computer screens are flashing non-stop.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:44.650
It's a tense environment in one way,

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:47.540
everybody's trying to manage
their profits to keep their jobs.

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:51.288
Suddenly, I hear a voice.

00:00:51.288 --> 00:00:57.164
The S&amp;P is falling,
where can I sell $50 million?

00:00:57.164 --> 00:00:59.043
It's a chaotic environment,

00:00:59.043 --> 00:01:02.665
my heart beats rapidly as I
desperately try to keep my cool.

00:01:02.665 --> 00:01:08.185
Finally, the clock strikes 5 PM,
and I breathe a sigh of

00:01:08.185 --> 00:01:13.821
relief as I manage to leave
the trading floor in one piece.

00:01:17.652 --> 00:01:21.192
As I sit in traffic on my way home,
I feel anxious, and

00:01:21.192 --> 00:01:23.970
struggle to focus as my mind wanders off.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:28.603
And after sitting there for a couple
of minutes, I notice a well-dressed

00:01:28.603 --> 00:01:33.040
middle-aged man trying to cross a one way
road with no traffic lights in sight.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.570
And, I think to myself, how strange,
what's he doing in the middle of the road?

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.470
I observe as he makes his way.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:44.280
And as I get closer,
I notice that he has a cane.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:47.080
I notice he's actually blind.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.958
So I immediately jump out of my car and
offer my assistance and

00:01:49.958 --> 00:01:51.940
ask him whether he needs a ride anywhere.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:57.446
And to my surprise,
without any hesitation, he smiles at me,

00:01:57.446 --> 00:02:01.990
introduces himself as Eustace,
and gives me his destination.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:09.062
It wasn't a very long ride, but
one with interesting conversation.

00:02:09.062 --> 00:02:09.930
I asked the man,

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:13.780
how do you manage to keep your composure
when in such a tense environment?

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:18.500
And then he proceeded to tell me a story
about how he had not always been blind,

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.985
and how losing his sight led him to pay
more attention to his surroundings.

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:23.680
He told me it had been a long journey for

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.450
him letting go of the resentment
that he had towards the world and

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.250
towards the circumstances that
led to him losing his sight.

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:32.898
And through that process,
he learned how to remain calm,

00:02:32.898 --> 00:02:35.587
especially when he felt lost or insecure.

00:02:35.587 --> 00:02:39.050
I had to learn how to focus
on what was happening now.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:41.450
How else would I find my way?

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:44.410
That's what he told me.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:48.017
In that moment.

00:02:50.292 --> 00:02:56.306
In that very moment,
I came to a sudden realization.

00:02:56.306 --> 00:03:02.078
Let go, focus, and listen.

00:03:02.078 --> 00:03:04.830
You see, like that man,
I was stuck in my past.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:07.710
I let my anxiety take control of me as
I imagined worst case scenarios for

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:10.310
my future based off previous
economic downturns.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:14.230
Do you ever feel as though you're
the person in the middle of the road with

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:15.380
tons of cars honking at you?

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:20.327
I said, me too.

00:03:20.327 --> 00:03:24.000
Life's events often approach us in the
form of fast-moving cars on a busy road,

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.490
leaving us feeling lost and insecure.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.841
And these feelings are amplified
as we ruminate on our past or

00:03:29.841 --> 00:03:31.694
on uncertainties in our future.

00:03:33.226 --> 00:03:36.636
How are we able to navigate through
the noise that life presents unless we're

00:03:36.636 --> 00:03:39.789
better able to manage our thoughts and
focus on what matters most to us?

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:45.230
As I gave more thought to the man's words,

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:47.850
I reflected upon a concept
called mindfulness.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:52.540
Mindfulness is by no means a new topic.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:55.010
In fact, it's been around for
many, many years.

00:03:57.550 --> 00:03:59.520
It's letting go of our
thoughts on the past and

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:03.039
future and being in increased awareness to
the present moment in our everyday lives.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:06.510
It's been shown to have
a number of benefits, and

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:08.690
it's practiced by some of
the largest companies in the world.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.880
General Mills instituted a seven-week
mindfulness program for its employees.

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:18.460
The program aims to get its leaders
to develop their mind's capacity,

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:21.049
to pay attention both to themselves and
to others.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.480
The program is usually successful.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:26.080
In fact, 80% of senior executives
have reported improved

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:28.230
decision-making skills
after taking the class.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:33.580
Google has a now-famous mindfulness
course called Search Within Yourself.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:36.380
It's a rock solid, business friendly,

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:40.250
mindfulness program composed of practical
ways to develop three traits, train your

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:45.018
attention, develop self-awareness,
and create useful mental habits.

00:04:45.018 --> 00:04:47.170
The program's oversubscribed and

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:49.590
has a minimum waiting time
of no fewer than six months.

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:53.120
But how does mindfulness
make us better leaders?

00:04:54.328 --> 00:04:58.028
Well, there are many ways, but what
Eustace learned that day was how to not

00:04:58.028 --> 00:05:01.379
let his negative thoughts from his
past affect his present moment or

00:05:01.379 --> 00:05:02.995
his perceptions of the future.

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:14.729
Better managing our thoughts extends to
a business context through a phenomenon

00:05:14.729 --> 00:05:16.200
called sunk cost bias.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:19.630
This is a bias that occurs
when one has invested so

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:22.700
much into a hopeless project that they
cannot bring themself to stop for

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:24.505
the fear of losing all that was invested.

00:05:24.505 --> 00:05:28.100
Sunk cost bias leads to future
irrational business decisions.

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:31.890
Let's think about that for a second.

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:37.892
Imagine you enter an ice cream shop and
purchase an expensive,

00:05:37.892 --> 00:05:41.706
elaborate double chocolate
peanut butter ice cream.

00:05:41.706 --> 00:05:44.990
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Halfway through eating it,

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:45.865
you start to feel sick.

00:05:45.865 --> 00:05:49.650
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Do you throw the remainder away?

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:51.003
Or do you feel obliged to finish it?

00:05:51.003 --> 00:05:55.164
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Only because you spent so

00:05:55.164 --> 00:05:56.430
much money on it already?

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:02.870
Devouring that remaining bit of ice
cream won't bring your money back.

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:08.216
In fact, you might have more to lose
in the form of lost pleasures or

00:06:08.216 --> 00:06:10.513
increased glucose levels.

00:06:10.513 --> 00:06:15.107
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Sunk cost bias is a thinking trap,

00:06:15.107 --> 00:06:19.279
and practicing mindfulness reduces
the tendency to allow prior unrecoverable

00:06:19.279 --> 00:06:21.159
costs to affect future decisions.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:27.280
The consequences of not better managing
our thoughts extends beyond a individual

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:29.200
or corporate level to a country level.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:34.430
I grew up in an apartheid-troubled
South Africa where segregation was rife.

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:38.850
The effects of this environment were
damaging to the emotional state of

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:43.080
the population, with the consequences
still prevailing today,

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:44.180
21 years after democracy.

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:51.040
South Africa's population is worried that
its future will end up like its past,

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:56.198
leading to a mindset that promotes social
divide and impacts economic performance.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:03.894
Ever since I met Eustace, to me being more
mindful has meant being able to let go,

00:07:03.894 --> 00:07:05.079
focus, and listen.

00:07:05.079 --> 00:07:09.042
It's the ability to let go
of my regrets in the past or

00:07:09.042 --> 00:07:13.907
my anxiety in the future and
to take a moment to focus and listen,

00:07:13.907 --> 00:07:16.810
truly listen to myself and to others.

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:20.716
Think about the last time you were
in conversation with someone, but

00:07:20.716 --> 00:07:22.780
you struggled to focus or
listen to what they were saying.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:27.212
Maybe you were thinking about
what to say in response,

00:07:27.212 --> 00:07:30.690
judging what they were saying, or
maybe you were daydreaming altogether.

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:37.180
Sometimes we're even too busy to listen to
our own inner thoughts, needs, or beliefs.

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:41.708
When was the last time
you listened to yourself?

00:07:46.213 --> 00:07:49.005
Fast forward a couple of months,
I'm back on the trading floor, but

00:07:49.005 --> 00:07:51.330
this time with a different mindset.

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:53.530
Let go, focus, and listen.

00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:58.099
I let go of the anxiety that I had towards
the uncontrollable events in my life.

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:02.890
One tool that I found useful was that
I started reframing certain situations

00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:05.540
as opportunities to grow rather
than as chances to fail.

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:10.460
So instead of thinking a poor performance
today could lead to me losing my

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:11.600
job, I thought,

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:15.030
I have a fantastic opportunity to help my
clients through difficult economic times.

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:19.400
I found I was a lot more energized for
the task at hand, and

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:20.929
I was actually better at my job.

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:25.910
I started to focus more on
the positives in my life,

00:08:25.910 --> 00:08:28.130
including those little things that
we sometimes take for granted.

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:32.570
So for example,
when driving home from work,

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:35.580
I thought of a few things that I was
grateful for, as opposed to replaying

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:37.560
the frustrating moments that I
experienced throughout the day.

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:43.760
This increased my levels of happiness and
made me a better colleague.

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:47.460
And finally, I listened to myself.

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:53.270
As I reflected, I realized that throughout
my life I'd been too results-focused,

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:55.460
especially on the trading floor.

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:57.640
And I never really took the time
to fully appreciate what I learned

00:08:57.640 --> 00:08:58.239
throughout the journey.

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:03.526
I started putting mini pauses between
my actions, which cleared my mind and

00:09:03.526 --> 00:09:05.999
allowed me to focus more on what
I was doing in that moment.

00:09:10.865 --> 00:09:13.279
I will likely never meet Eustace again,
but

00:09:13.279 --> 00:09:16.290
I'll never forget the lesson
that I learned that day.

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:22.700
Life rarely resembles walking
on a clear or straight path, but

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:24.820
rather looks like being
blindfolded on a busy road.

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:29.450
That's scary.

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:37.279
So the next time you find yourself in
the middle of the road with dozens of

00:09:37.279 --> 00:09:39.510
cars honking at you, remember.

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:44.000
Let go, focus, and listen.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:49.410
Listen to those around you,
and listen to your heart.

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:50.455
Thank you.

00:09:50.455 --> 00:09:54.356
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:54.356 --> 00:09:58.829
[MUSIC]

